# Copy to .env — ONE file. It used to say ".env.local", and for a while the repo
# genuinely had both: five keys lived in .env.local, which overrides .env, and one
# of them (NEXT_PUBLIC_DEFAULT_MEET_BASE) silently disagreed with .env — so every
# live class room was named after the old repo instead of the product, and nothing
# anywhere reported it. Two files with override precedence is a split-brain waiting
# for one key to drift. .env.local is still read (Next's precedence, and the CLI
# scripts follow it) but there is no longer a reason to create one.
#
# BOTH apps read this one file from the repo root (apps/web via next.config.mjs,
# apps/api via src/env.ts, the CLI scripts via packages/db/bin/_paths.ts). A real
# environment variable always wins over the file.
#
# `npm run db:check` reports which keys are set / empty / missing (never a value).
#
# IN DEVELOPMENT `npm run dev` refuses to start without API_URL. To see the UI with
# no backend at all, opt in to demo data: TNAJEM_DEMO=1 npm run dev (fictitious
# tutors, a "MODE DÉMO" banner on every page).
#
# IN PRODUCTION there is no demo data, ever: the web server refuses to start
# without API_URL or with TNAJEM_DEMO=1 (apps/web/scripts/preflight.mjs), and the
# API refuses to boot without AUTH_SECRET.

# --- Postgres (server-side only; when set, the app uses the real DB) ---
# No Docker needed — point this at EITHER:
#   • your existing local Postgres:  postgresql://postgres:YOUR_PASSWORD@localhost:5432/tnajem  (CREATE DATABASE tnajem; first)
#   • a free cloud DB (Neon):        paste the connection string from your Neon project
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/tnajem

# --- Postgres tuning (optional — the defaults are correct for a single VPS) ---
# Read by packages/db/src/client.ts. See DEPLOY.md §3 and SCALABILITY.md §2.
#
# DB_POOL_MAX — pool size PER NODE PROCESS (default 10). What Postgres actually
#   sees is (pm2 workers × DB_POOL_MAX). Stock Postgres allows 100 connections,
#   minus 3 superuser-reserved, minus ~10 for psql/backups/the purge cron → keep
#   the product UNDER ~87. 1 worker = 10; the recommended 2 workers = 20. If you
#   add workers, LOWER this. A bigger pool is not how you scale (PgBouncer is).
# DB_POOL_MAX=10
#
# DB_PREPARE — server-side prepared statements (default ON; only "0" disables).
#   A real win on the hot point-lookups (sessions.token, tutors.slug). Set to 0 in
#   the SAME change that puts PgBouncer in transaction-pooling mode in front of
#   Postgres — a prepared statement lives on a server connection the next
#   transaction may not get back. Not before.
# DB_PREPARE=1
#
# DB_SSL — auto-detected: TLS required for a REMOTE database, skipped over the
#   loopback (a default local Postgres does not offer TLS at all). Only set this
#   if you move Postgres off the box. Values: require | 0
# DB_SSL=require

# --- Public site URL (canonical origin) ---
# Used by metadataBase, apps/web/app/robots.ts and the sitemap. Defaults to https://tnajem.com
# when unset. Set it per-environment (e.g. a preview/staging URL) so preview
# deploys don't emit canonical links + a sitemap pointing at production.
# The API reads it too: the links in booking and reminder emails (apps/api/src/lib/booking-mail.ts).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://tnajem.com

# --- Auth (sessions + OTP) ---
# Secret every OTP hash is derived from: sha256(`identifier:code:AUTH_SECRET`),
# where the identifier is the e-mail address (or the phone, under OTP_CHANNEL=sms).
#
# REQUIRED AT RUNTIME IN PRODUCTION — with NODE_ENV=production and this unset,
# packages/shared/src/auth-core.ts logs a FATAL CONFIG line on boot and then THROWS on the first OTP
# hash, so every login fails loudly. That is deliberate: the dev default below is
# public in this repo, so with it in place an attacker can precompute the hash of
# all 1,000,000 codes for a phone offline, and the 5-attempt budget stops meaning
# anything. A silent fallback would ship guessable-hash auth and nobody would find out.
#
# NOTE: it is resolved LAZILY (on first use), NOT at module load — so it is NOT
# needed to run `next build`. Do not "fix" that by throwing at import time: every
# page's server bundle imports lib/auth (SiteShell → SiteHeader → app/actions), so
# a module-load throw kills the build on any box that injects secrets at runtime.
#
# Generate a real one, EVEN FOR DEVELOPMENT: openssl rand -hex 32
# This file used to ship the public dev default here, so every copied .env ran on
# a secret printed in the repo. Leave it empty until you paste your own —
# `npm run db:check` rejects the public default.
AUTH_SECRET=
# --- OTP channel ---
# Where a login code is sent. "email" (default) or "sms".
#
# This is the ENTIRE revert path. The SMS implementation is still live and
# compiling in packages/shared/src/sms.ts and apps/web/app/actions.ts — it was not commented out, because
# commented code is invisible to tsc and to the audit gates and rots the moment
# anything around it moves. Set this to "sms" and restart to go back to phone
# login; no rebuild, no code change.
#
# Note it also selects the ADMIN allowlist below (ADMIN_EMAILS vs ADMIN_PHONES),
# because the two channels store their identity in different columns.
OTP_CHANNEL=email

# --- Email (OTP delivery over SMTP, via nodemailer) ---
# Login codes are emailed only when HOST + USER + PASS + FROM_ADDRESS are all set;
# otherwise the code is shown on-screen (dev mode only — never once a provider is
# configured, see requestOtp). Swap providers by changing these values, not code.
#
# GMAIL NOTES, learned the hard way:
#  • MAIL_PASS must be an APP PASSWORD (Google Account → Security → App passwords),
#    not the account password. 2FA has to be on for that menu to exist.
#  • MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS should EQUAL MAIL_USER. Gmail rewrites the From header to
#    the authenticated account unless the address is a verified alias, so a
#    mismatch silently ships mail that says something other than what you set.
#  • A free gmail.com account is capped around 500 recipients/day (Workspace:
#    2000). That is a real ceiling for a launch expecting signups — every login
#    attempt spends one. Move to a transactional provider before it bites.
#  • Port 465 = implicit TLS (MAIL_SECURE=true). Port 587 = STARTTLS (false).
#    Leave MAIL_SECURE unset and packages/shared/src/mail.ts infers it from the port.
# MAIL_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# MAIL_PORT=465
# MAIL_SECURE=true
# MAIL_USER=support@example.com
# MAIL_PASS=xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# MAIL_FROM_NAME=Tnajem
# MAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=support@example.com
# MAIL_REPLY_TO=support@example.com

# --- SMS (the OTP_CHANNEL=sms path; via Twilio REST — no SDK needed) ---
# Also the optional second channel for notify(): booking confirmations and
# verification decisions are texted when a profile has a phone on file. Since
# email became the login identity, that number is collected during onboarding
# (/student/welcome, /onboarding) and is optional — so this stays useful even
# while OTP_CHANNEL=email.
# Login codes are texted
# only when SID + TOKEN + (FROM *or* MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID) are all set;
# otherwise the code is shown on-screen (dev mode only — never in production).
# For Tunisia, a Messaging Service with a registered alphanumeric sender ID
# usually delivers better than a US long code. Set ONE of FROM / MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID.
# Swap for a Tunisian gateway by editing packages/shared/src/sms.ts (provider-agnostic).
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
# TWILIO_FROM=+1XXXXXXXXXX                 # a Twilio number (delivers to +216), OR:
# TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID=MGxxxxxxxx  # recommended: Messaging Service w/ sender ID

# --- Admin (tutor verification review) ---
# Who may open /admin/verifications and approve/reject tutors. Empty = NOBODY is
# admin — the gate fails closed, deliberately: that queue holds every uploaded
# national ID scan.
#
# Comma-separated, same format as the login identity.
#
# ADMIN_PHONES IS GONE. It used to be the allowlist for OTP_CHANNEL=sms, and the
# pair was documented as "set the one that matches your channel" — but nothing has
# read it since the consolidation in Step 0, so a number there granted no access at
# all while looking exactly like it should. If phone login ever comes back, add the
# key AND the code that reads it in the same change.
ADMIN_EMAILS=

# --- Storage (uploaded verification documents — SENSITIVE) ---
# Tutors upload national ID scans here. Under Tunisia's INPDP regime (Loi 2004-63)
# these are sensitive personal data: keep them off the public web root and out of git.
#
# Dev: defaults to a local ./.storage folder (gitignored).
# PROD: MUST be a PERSISTENT VOLUME (e.g. a Render disk mounted at /var/data).
#   On an ephemeral filesystem the ID docs vanish on every redeploy, which both
#   breaks the admin review queue and leaves orphaned verification_docs rows
#   pointing at files that no longer exist. Back this folder up with Postgres.
# STORAGE_DIR=/var/data

# --- Retention purge (see packages/db/src/retention.ts) ---
# ONE daily job, FOUR independent things swept (independent: each runs whether or
# not the others succeeded). The first two:
#   1. ID documents past the 90-day window. /privacy promises identity documents
#      are deleted at most 90 days after the verification decision — nothing
#      enforces that unless the purge actually runs.
#   2. Expired sessions + otp_codes. Both tables grow by one row per login / per
#      OTP request, forever; nothing else ever deletes them.
#   3. Accounts whose 30-day deletion grace has expired (Step 15). Without this,
#      "supprimer mon compte" never actually deletes anything.
#   4. Subscriptions past their expiry (Step 16) — bookkeeping only; the
#      entitlement resolver already treats a past expiry as dead.
#
# Both entry points run the SAME four jobs (runRetention in retention.ts):
# Run it EITHER as a cron'd CLI ....... npm run db:purge          (add -- --dry-run to preview)
#                 OR as an HTTP cron ... POST /cron/purge ON THE API (port 4000)
#                                        with "Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET".
#                                        NOTE: it was /api/cron/purge on the WEB app
#                                        before Step 5. That route no longer exists —
#                                        an old crontab 404s every night in silence
#                                        while /privacy keeps promising deletion.
#
# The SAME secret protects POST /cron/reminders (class reminders + review prompts,
# every 10 minutes — DEPLOY.md §7.1).
#
# CRON_SECRET protects that route. Generate one: openssl rand -hex 32
# If it is unset, /cron/purge refuses to run (503) rather than exposing an
# unauthenticated destructive endpoint. In production the API refuses to START
# without it, and docker compose refuses to bring the stack up.
CRON_SECRET=

# --- Payments (money movement — OFF until legal sign-off) ---
# Master switch, read by paymentsEnabled() in packages/shared/src/payments.ts. ONLY "1" enables it;
# unset/empty = OFF, so an env typo can never start moving money.
# While OFF: every payment adapter method throws, and the tutor balance is a real 0
# (we never fabricate earnings). Do NOT set this to 1 until counsel signs off AND
# the provider contract + webhook signature verification actually exist.
PAYMENTS_ENABLED=

# --- Minors (phase-a lane L2 · A24, decision D6: adult-only pilot) ---
# Read per call by minorsAllowed() in packages/shared/src/age.ts, by the API AND the web.
# ONLY "1" lets under-18s sign up and book (with guardian consent); unset/empty = adults only.
# Keep it OFF until Phase D (parent sign-up, parent reporting, parent-confirmed consent).
ALLOW_MINORS=

# --- Live video (MVP: a default Jitsi room base, or per-class links) ---
# The base each class's private room token is appended to. It MUST be an absolute
# https:// URL prefix (scheme, host, then a path). Unset or EMPTY means the built-in
# default, https://meet.jit.si/tnajem- (packages/shared/src/live.ts); anything else
# that is not https:// is ignored for that same default, and
# `npm run db:check -- --production` fails on it. Baked into the web bundle at build
# time, and read by the API at runtime: change it, then rebuild and restart both.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEFAULT_MEET_BASE=https://meet.jit.si/tnajem-

# --- Support WhatsApp (the "Aide & support" row on /account) ---
# InnoviaBurst's business number, written the way wa.me wants it: country code +
# number, DIGITS ONLY, no "+" and no spaces. Unset, empty or anything that is not
# 8-15 digits HIDES the row (packages/shared/src/support-contact.ts). Baked into
# the web bundle at build time, like every NEXT_PUBLIC_ value.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPPORT_WHATSAPP=

# --- LATER (gated on legal sign-off — DO NOT wire until counsel confirms) ---
# KONNECT_API_KEY=
# KONNECT_WALLET_ID=
# FLOUCI_APP_TOKEN=
# FLOUCI_APP_SECRET=
# --- LATER (WhatsApp reminders) ---
# WHATSAPP_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_ID=

# ── Document store ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STORAGE_DIR is REQUIRED once this is a monorepo, in dev as well as prod.
#
# The dev fallback is `join(process.cwd(), ".storage")`, and cwd now depends on
# WHICH WORKSPACE ran the command: `npm run db:purge` executes inside
# packages/db, `next build` inside apps/web, and Next's standalone server.js
# chdir()s to its own folder. Leave it unset and the purge looks in
# packages/db/.storage while the ID scans sit in apps/web/.storage.
#
# That is not a cosmetic mismatch. The purge treats a missing file as non-fatal:
# it counts the document "already gone" and DELETES THE ROW ANYWAY. A job with
# the wrong base directory therefore deletes every row, orphans every national
# ID scan on disk, and reports success. Set it explicitly, always.
# In production it must be a PERSISTENT volume, and storageBase() throws rather
# than guess.
STORAGE_DIR=/var/lib/tnajem/storage
# Which object store every upload goes through (packages/db/src/storage.ts):
#   local  files under STORAGE_DIR (default)
#   s3     any S3-compatible bucket: AWS S3, Cloudflare R2, MinIO
# Anything else, or s3 with a required key missing, stops the API at boot; there
# is never a silent fallback to local disk. DEPLOY.md §3 has the bucket policy.
# STORAGE_DRIVER=local
#
# Required with STORAGE_DRIVER=s3. The bucket MUST be private.
# S3_BUCKET=
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# Optional with STORAGE_DRIVER=s3:
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com   # unset for AWS S3
# S3_REGION=us-east-1          # AWS: the bucket's real region. R2/MinIO: leave it.
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true     # MinIO needs it; AWS and R2 do not
# S3_PREFIX=                   # e.g. "prod" to share a bucket with staging

# ── Identity documents: encryption at rest (DEPLOY.md §3) ────────────────────
# Every verification document (ID scans, selfies, diplomas) is sealed with
# AES-256-GCM before it is stored. 32 bytes: openssl rand -hex 32
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION — the API refuses to start without it. Without it in
# development, documents are stored unencrypted, with a warning.
# LOSING THIS KEY MAKES EVERY STORED ID SCAN UNREADABLE. Keep it in your secret
# store, never alongside the backups of the files it protects.
DOC_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Only during a key rotation: the OLD key, which can still open documents.
# Run npm run db:encrypt-docs, then remove this line.
# DOC_ENCRYPTION_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# ── Backups (npm run db:backup / db:restore — DEPLOY.md §8) ─────────────────
# Where dumps + manifests go. Default ./backups (git-ignored). They hold every
# user's personal data: encrypt and ship them off the machine.
# BACKUP_DIR=/var/backups/tnajem/db
# Folder holding pg_dump / pg_restore, if they are not on PATH. Client tools must
# be at the server's major version or newer (18).
# PG_BIN=/usr/lib/postgresql/18/bin
# Set ONLY for a restore, to a NEW, EMPTY database. db:restore refuses the
# DATABASE_URL database and any database that already has tables.
# RESTORE_DATABASE_URL=

# ── API (Step 3) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
API_PORT=4000
API_HOST=127.0.0.1
# Server-side only. NOT NEXT_PUBLIC_: that would bake an internal hostname into
# the client bundle, where the browser cannot resolve it.
API_URL=http://127.0.0.1:4000

# Demo data (development only). "1" serves fictitious tutors when API_URL is
# empty, with a banner on every page. Never set this in production — the web
# server refuses to start with it.
# TNAJEM_DEMO=1

# EXACT origin allow-list, comma separated. Never "*", and never reflect the
# request's Origin header — credentials here are cookies, so reflecting the
# origin hands any site on the internet the ability to make authenticated
# requests as the logged-in user. Empty in production = the API refuses to boot.
CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# Scope the session cookie so tnajem.com and api.tnajem.com share it. Leave UNSET
# in dev: both are localhost, where a domain attribute breaks the cookie.
# COOKIE_DOMAIN=.tnajem.com

# Which upstream hops may set X-Forwarded-For. Comma separated addresses — the
# web app / reverse proxy, and nothing else.
#
# This is the highest-severity setting in the split. clientIp() keys the OTP
# limiter, so once the browser no longer talks to the process that throttles it:
#   unset in prod -> every user shares ONE bucket (degraded, but unforgeable)
#   trust-all     -> X-Forwarded-For becomes attacker-controlled and the per-IP
#                    limiter is bypassed by rotating a header. Never do this.
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION: the API refuses to boot without it, and refuses any
# value that trusts every hop ("true", "*", 0.0.0.0/0, ::/0).
# TRUSTED_PROXIES=10.0.0.5

# The API runs with every production rule ON unless NODE_ENV says otherwise:
# unset means production. `npm run dev` sets development for you. Set it
# explicitly on a server anyway, so nothing else on the box has to guess.
# NODE_ENV=production

# --- Logging (optional) ---
# Fastify's pino level for apps/api. Default: "info" (production) / "debug" (dev).
# Values: fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace | silent
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Error tracking (optional — OBSERVABILITY.md) ---
# UNSET MEANS OFF, in every environment, and off is the default. With no DSN the
# SDK is never initialised: nothing is sent, nothing is buffered, and both servers
# behave exactly as they did before Stage 7.
#
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY, and that is a decision, not an omission. Browser errors are not
# reported: the site's CSP `connect-src` is 'self' + Cloudflare's analytics endpoint
# only (apps/web/next.config.mjs), so a
# browser SDK would mean widening it to an external ingest origin and shipping more
# JavaScript to every page over 3G. Do not add a NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN without
# making that trade deliberately.
#
# What is sent: the exception and its stack, the route PATTERN, the method, the
# status, and the request id. Never a cookie, a body, a query string, an e-mail or
# a phone number — packages/shared/src/observability.ts holds the policy and the
# scrubber, and apps/api/test/sentry.test.ts proves it against the same key list
# the log redaction uses.
#
# The DSN is not a secret in the cryptographic sense (it is a public ingest key),
# but it identifies the project and anyone holding it can send events to it: keep
# it in the secret store with everything else, and never log it.
# SENTRY_DSN=https://<key>@<org>.ingest.sentry.io/<project>
#
# Tags every event. Defaults to NODE_ENV (and unset NODE_ENV means production).
# Set it explicitly on a staging box so its errors do not read as production ones.
# SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=production
#
# Which deploy an error came from. deploy.sh exports the commit sha automatically;
# set it by hand only when running outside that script. Falls back to
# "tnajem@<package version>", which groups every deploy of 0.1.0 together.
# SENTRY_RELEASE=abc1234

# --- Payment rail (optional; inert while PAYMENTS_ENABLED is off) ---
# Which adapter paymentProvider() returns: "konnect" (default) or "flouci". Every
# method on both throws until PAYMENTS_ENABLED=1, so this changes nothing today.
# PAYMENTS_PROVIDER=konnect
